WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:05.200
hi
it's michelle from lab muffin beauty

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:08.160
science here chemistry phd and skin care
nerd

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.240
i talk about sunscreen a lot here but
sunscreen isn't the only way to protect

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.799
your skin
so today i'm going to be talking about

00:00:14.799 --> 00:00:17.600
how to choose clothes to protect your
skin from uv

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.199
us australians were brought up with
these slogans if you were born in the

00:00:21.199 --> 00:00:24.400
80s and 90s then you know the original
slip slop slap

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.480
this means slip on a shirt slop on the
spf 50 plus sunscreen

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.399
and slap on a hat if you're a young
whipper snapper who needs to get off my

00:00:32.399 --> 00:00:35.920
lawn with your fortnite dancers
then you know the two extra ones which

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:40.000
are seek shade and
slide on some sunglasses so let's talk

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.680
about uv protective clothing
what's it all about and what clothes

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.640
should you be wearing
if you like this sort of nerdy video

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:50.320
give it a thumbs up subscribe and click
the notification bell so you don't miss

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.960
any videos

00:00:56.239 --> 00:01:00.399
i think uv protective clothing is really
underrated in skincare

00:01:00.399 --> 00:01:04.479
there are a lot of advantages to using
clothes as your protection against uv

00:01:04.479 --> 00:01:07.920
rather than using sunscreen
firstly you don't need to reapply your

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.640
clothes you put them on and they're
going to stay there

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:15.520
you also can't well it's very difficult
to incorrectly apply your clothes

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:19.840
with sunscreen it's very easy you might
miss a spot you might apply too little

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:23.439
clothing is also by default broad
spectrum it protects against uva as well

00:01:23.439 --> 00:01:26.560
as visible light
there are also a lot of disadvantages to

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:30.079
uv protective clothing
firstly obviously you can't cover

00:01:30.079 --> 00:01:34.079
everything unless you radically change
your fashion so for most of us we can't

00:01:34.079 --> 00:01:37.759
cover our face our ears
and generally our hands as well even if

00:01:37.759 --> 00:01:41.200
you try to protect your face with a hat
the problem is you still get that

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:44.320
diffuse uv even though you're not
getting direct uv that's coming straight

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:47.920
at you from the sun
uv bounces against other things and that

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:51.040
bounces onto your face
there's also the fact that summer is

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:55.360
really hot so the time where you're most
likely to get dangerous levels of uv

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.960
exposure is also the time where you
don't feel like wearing as many clothes

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.280
finding uv protective clothing is also
really tricky which is why i'm going to

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:09.840
break it down in this video

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:14.640
upf is ultraviolet protection factor and
it's the most reliable way to tell how

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.040
much uv protection you're getting from
clothes

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:21.200
upf for clothing is just like spf for
sunscreen if you're wearing a piece of

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:25.840
upf 50 clothing that means only 1/50th
of the sunburn causing

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:30.480
or erythemal uv is going to reach your
skin just like with spf the higher the

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.599
upf number the more uv protection you're
getting

00:02:33.599 --> 00:02:38.959
the difference is with spf and sunscreen
that only happens if you apply two

00:02:38.959 --> 00:02:42.560
milligrams per square centimeter so if
you apply the same amount that they use

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:45.680
in spf testing
most people don't apply this much and

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:49.040
it's really hard to get this much evenly
all over your skin

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:52.560
with upf what you see on the label is
what you get when you put on your

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:54.800
clothing
although this can change over the

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:58.879
lifetime of your clothing which we'll
talk a bit about later the upf rating

00:02:58.879 --> 00:03:02.400
for a piece of clothing is determined by
a standardized test

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.440
scientists measure how much uv passes
through the fabric

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:09.680
this is called transmittance and this is
done without using human skin so it's an

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:13.680
in vitro test
erythemal or sunburn causing uv is a

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:17.200
mixture of uvb
and a little bit of uva light so what

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:19.920
scientists do is they measure the uva
and uvb

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.239
transmittance separately and then they
use maths to work out how much will get

00:03:24.239 --> 00:03:27.440
through the clothing to burn your skin
in general you actually get higher

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:31.360
protection from your upf clothing than
what the upf rating is

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:35.120
that's because upf is sort of like a
worst case scenario where

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:39.040
all of the uv is coming straight on into
your clothing through the pores

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:43.440
in real life clothing is 3d so if the
light is coming in this way

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:46.480
only this bit is going to get that
straight on um

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:50.560
uv whereas on your other bits it doesn't
go straight through and so you get a lot

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:53.840
more protection
obviously the upf rating is just for the

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:57.439
skin covered so if you're wearing upf
clothing and your fingers are exposed

00:03:57.439 --> 00:03:59.840
you're not going to get any protection
on your fingers

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:04.159
there are also regulations around what
sorts of clothing can get upf ratings

00:04:04.159 --> 00:04:07.680
so generally it has to cover enough skin
so for example

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:11.680
singlets and bikinis generally aren't
allowed to have upf ratings

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:15.680
some places also have a limit on the
highest upf you can claim just like with

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:18.880
sunscreen in australia the highest upf
you can claim

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:26.320
is 50 plus just like with spf

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:30.080
so with upf clothing you know what
protection you can expect

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:34.000
not many clothes actually have upf
ratings but regular clothing can also

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:38.240
protect your skin from uv
it just isn't measured and we know this

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:40.960
because
of course most of us have gotten tan

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:44.639
lines at some point from clothes
unfortunately a lot of fabrics don't

00:04:44.639 --> 00:04:48.479
actually provide that much uv protection
so in one study they found that

00:04:48.479 --> 00:04:51.600
one-third of the clothes they looked at
one-third of the summer clothes

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:56.160
were actually less than upf-15 less than
half of the clothes they looked at had

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:59.360
greater than upf 30.
it can be really hard to compare

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:02.800
different fabrics because there are so
many variables that will change how much

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:06.720
uv will go through
that's why upf rated clothing is so good

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:09.759
in my opinion
because you know for sure how much uv

00:05:09.759 --> 00:05:12.320
protection you're getting
so i'm going to talk about some of the

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:14.240
different variables that you need to
look at

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:18.000
and generally what you want to do is
just tick off as many boxes as you can

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:22.000
the type of material that the fabric's
made from makes a huge difference

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:25.520
natural fibers in general just aren't
very uv absorbent so i'm talking about

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:30.240
things like cotton
rayon viscose and linen in one study all

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:34.479
of the white cotton rayon and linen that
they tested were below upf 5

00:05:34.479 --> 00:05:38.560
even if you have really tightly woven
heavy natural fabrics a lot of the time

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:42.479
the upf that you get
is less than 20. wool nylon and silk are

00:05:42.479 --> 00:05:46.000
better
but the best one is polyester polyester

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:49.440
is really good at absorbing uvb
because of its structure it's got

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:52.479
benzene rings which is similar to some
sunscreens

00:05:52.479 --> 00:05:56.240
when comparing similar fabrics made of
polyester and cotton so similar weave

00:05:56.240 --> 00:05:59.440
similar thickness
they found that in general polyester had

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:03.680
three to four times the upf
of the cotton fabrics the big problem is

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:06.479
that polyester is awful to wear in
summer

00:06:06.479 --> 00:06:10.639
so it gets very sweaty it's not
breathable it's just a bit gross

00:06:10.639 --> 00:06:14.560
but you can get blends of polyester with
other materials and so this becomes a

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:19.120
lot more comfortable and more breathable
the uv protection does get a bit diluted

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:21.919
but it's just a lot more practical to
wear

00:06:21.919 --> 00:06:25.280
so this happens with things like newer
generation sports clothes

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:29.120
with newer generation sports materials
you can get lots of little layers of

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:32.319
fabric
in the same fabric and so you get a lot

00:06:32.319 --> 00:06:35.440
higher upf
so for example some professional soccer

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:40.240
shirts are greater than upf 100
fabric isn't solid and so uv gets

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:42.720
through little holes little pores in the
fabric

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:46.319
so if you have a fabric with smaller
holes or less holes you're going to get

00:06:46.319 --> 00:06:49.039
a higher uv
protection regardless of what material

00:06:49.039 --> 00:06:52.560
you're using so there are a whole bunch
of factors that affect how big and how

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:55.599
many holes you get
firstly there's the thickness of the

00:06:55.599 --> 00:06:58.240
fibers with thicker fibers you end up
with

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:01.599
better uv protection there's also the
way that the yarn is spun

00:07:01.599 --> 00:07:06.000
with more twisted yarn you end up with a
more compact fabric so again

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:09.919
higher uv protection then there's also
the construction of the fabric so

00:07:09.919 --> 00:07:14.400
how that thread is turned into a fabric
knitted fabrics which have a little bit

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:17.199
of stretch to them
tend to be better than woven fabrics

00:07:17.199 --> 00:07:20.400
with no stretch so these are used for
things like business shirts

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:23.680
then there's also washing some fabrics
shrink a bit in the wash

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:27.120
and the holes shrink as well this
happens a lot with cotton but it's

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:29.759
really variable
so in some studies they've actually

00:07:29.759 --> 00:07:33.039
found that after 5-10 washes you get
double the upf

00:07:33.039 --> 00:07:36.319
on the other hand after you've washed
your clothes a bit too much they start

00:07:36.319 --> 00:07:39.039
losing threads and so they get thinner
and more threadbare

00:07:39.039 --> 00:07:42.400
the threads get skinnier and the holes
get bigger in general

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:45.759
heavier and thicker fabric will give you
more protection because of the size of

00:07:45.759 --> 00:07:48.639
the holes
and you can very roughly look through

00:07:48.639 --> 00:07:51.520
the fabric and see how much light is
getting through the fabric

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:56.639
to see how big the holes are with really
thick denim you can get a upf of 1700

00:07:56.639 --> 00:07:59.759
with a typical cotton summer t-shirt you
get a upf

00:07:59.759 --> 00:08:04.639
of about five to nine with something
like 15 denier stockings you get a upf

00:08:04.639 --> 00:08:07.680
of less than two
the color of your clothes also makes a

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:11.360
huge difference and this probably isn't
very surprising if you know what the

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:14.720
structure of a dye looks like
it looks a lot like a sunscreen with

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:17.919
lots of benzene rings
these benzene rings are actually how

00:08:17.919 --> 00:08:21.520
dyes act as dyes
so the benzene rings let them absorb

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:24.560
some visible light which means they
reflect a different colour of visible

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:28.000
light and that's the color you see
but the fact that they absorb some

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:30.639
visible light also means that they can
absorb uv

00:08:30.639 --> 00:08:34.479
light as well the denser the fabric the
greater this effect because

00:08:34.479 --> 00:08:38.560
obviously the dye is sitting in the
threads if you have a bleached fabric

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:42.159
then you're actually destroying any
natural dyes that are in the fabric

00:08:42.159 --> 00:08:46.959
and so the upf goes down if you have an
unbleached fabric the upf will be a bit

00:08:46.959 --> 00:08:49.440
higher
but the best thing you can do is get a

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:53.760
really intensely dyed fabric
in one study dyeing cotton fabrics

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:58.160
actually made the upf go up three times
in another study dyeing the fabric blue

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:01.279
made it go up five times
and dyeing it yellow made it go up

00:09:01.279 --> 00:09:05.839
double dyeing it blue was actually better
than using a special uv treatment on the

00:09:05.839 --> 00:09:08.720
fabric
the problem is in most of these studies

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:13.120
doing the same color
made the upf go up different amounts the

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:16.800
most important thing seems to be the
intensity of the dye so how much dye

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:20.000
you're getting into the fabric
regardless of what color it is

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:23.920
in general darker colors worked better
in most studies so things like black and

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:26.480
blue
but in other studies other colors worked

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:29.760
really well as well
so for example things like red and even

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:32.720
beige again
as you wear a piece of clothing more

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:35.279
this can change because the dye starts
fading

00:09:35.279 --> 00:09:38.959
and so again less dye means less
protection in general the types of

00:09:38.959 --> 00:09:41.600
fabrics you're looking for are the types
of fabric that you use

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:46.000
in things like polo shirts t-shirts
sports shirts and dresses

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:50.320
in general business shirts are not very
good and this is because of the colors

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:53.200
they tend to come in
and the fact that they're woven and thin

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:56.160
polyester blends are especially good
especially if they come

00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:00.240
in dark colors now on top of the type of
fabric you're using there are a few

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:02.560
other things that will change the upf
level

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:07.760
firstly there are uv treatments so it's
like putting sunscreen on your clothes

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:11.279
just like with sunscreen this comes in
both chemical and physical forms

00:10:11.279 --> 00:10:14.880
this can happen during the production of
the fabric so for example if the

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:18.399
manufacturer decides to choose polyester
and make a polyester blend

00:10:18.399 --> 00:10:21.440
they're adding some of that chemical
sunscreen from the polyester

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:24.480
you can also have physical sunscreen so
you can have embedded zinc

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:28.480
titanium and even nanodiamond there are
also treatments that you can add to your

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:32.640
clothes after you've bought them so the
most famous one is called rit sun guard

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:35.839
this contains a special sunscreening
agent called Tinosorb

00:10:35.839 --> 00:10:41.200
FD and after one wash you end up with
upf-30 clothing which lasts through 20

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:44.480
washes
this one works best with natural fabrics

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:47.360
there's also
stretching of the fabric so if you think

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:50.320
about the size of the holes in your
fabric the more you stretch it the

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:53.040
bigger the holes get and so the lower
the upf

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:56.640
how much uv protection you lose from
stretching depends a lot on the type of

00:10:56.640 --> 00:10:59.440
fabric so
the thickness of the fabric which way

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:03.920
you're stretching it but in general
you actually lose a lot of uv protection

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:07.760
with not very much stretch
so for example if you stretch a cotton

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:11.519
lycra blend by 15
you actually lose half of the upf

00:11:11.519 --> 00:11:14.480
protection if you stretch your stocking
by thirty percent

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:19.440
the upf actually goes to one ninth and
this isn't very much so this is what it

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:21.839
looks like so generally for sun
protection

00:11:21.839 --> 00:11:25.680
go for looser fitting clothing
especially if it's a stretchy material

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:29.600
with wet clothing you also tend to get
lower upf protection which you might

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:33.040
have already guessed if you've seen
a patch of clothing go see-through when

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:36.320
it's wet the problem is the uv
protection can also go

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:40.000
up when clothing's wet and it's pretty
much impossible to predict which way it

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:42.560
goes
in general cotton clothing tends to

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:47.680
decrease in uv protection when it's wet
so a dry t-shirt will be upf five to

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:50.880
nine
a wet t-shirt will be upf three to four

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:54.480
point five
but outside of that you pretty much

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:57.839
can't say either way

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:04.079
so if you're looking for uv protective
clothing what you want is

00:12:04.079 --> 00:12:08.240
synthetic fabrics tightly woven fabrics
with tiny holes

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:12.560
you also want dark intense colors and
you want them to cover as much of your

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:15.760
body as possible
unfortunately what this also means is

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:19.360
that it's probably not going to be very
nice to wear if you're out in the sun

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:23.600
and very active so i think that's the
big advantage of upf rated clothing

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:27.279
you can get things like special
technologies within the fibers or added

00:12:27.279 --> 00:12:30.320
to the fibers
and so you can find comfortable clothes

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:33.120
that will give you a guaranteed level of
uv protection

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:36.560
that means you can also cut down how
long it takes to reapply sunscreen

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:39.600
and you don't have to worry about
getting the sunscreen on hard to reach

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:45.360
places

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:48.399
so here are some brands of upf clothing
i've actually tried

00:12:48.399 --> 00:12:52.880
this is a hoodie from uniqlo's uv range
it's got really high uv protection so

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:56.880
upf 50 even though it's really light and
cool so they use some of their airism

00:12:56.880 --> 00:12:59.760
technology
so that it's really breezy on your skin

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:02.959
to get this really high protection
they've added special filters to the

00:13:02.959 --> 00:13:06.800
fibers that block and reflect uv
the problem with uniqlo is that there's

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:09.600
only about four upf rated pieces of
clothing

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:13.279
and so it's really limited although it
comes in like every color

00:13:13.279 --> 00:13:17.200
it's also really affordable so i think
this hoodie was about thirty dollars

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:21.519
so if i had to make one big complaint
about this is that the pocket size is

00:13:21.519 --> 00:13:24.480
really weird it doesn't quite fit your
phone in comfortably

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:28.560
you can sort of squish it in there and
it gets really snug which i guess is

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:30.560
good because then you're less likely to
drop it

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:34.320
there are also thumb holes in the sleeve
which is a really common feature with

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:36.959
upf
rated clothing it means that it protects

00:13:36.959 --> 00:13:40.800
the back of your hands a bit
the problem with this is that with most

00:13:40.800 --> 00:13:45.279
of these it's not grippy
and this is actually the hoodie i was

00:13:45.279 --> 00:13:47.600
wearing with
my thumb in the thumb hole when i

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:51.519
dropped my phone and cracked the screen
for the first time in one and a half

00:13:51.519 --> 00:13:54.959
years so
i kind of hate this hoodie but it is

00:13:54.959 --> 00:13:58.240
really protective
i also have this rash vest from mecca

00:13:58.240 --> 00:14:01.279
cosmetica
rash vests - when i think of rash vests i

00:14:01.279 --> 00:14:04.240
think of these really uncool awful
designs from the

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:09.120
90s so they're like fluoro um they have
this little like weird neck

00:14:09.120 --> 00:14:13.600
thing they have really awful patterns
and yeah pretty much the moment i turned

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:16.800
12 i was like right no more of this i'm
